                "tStart": 2124,
                "tEnd": 2533,
                "text": "Vaishnavi Kotiyal: Thank you, Beauti. So from here I'll take on the test scores. So, Aastha, can you go for the next slide? So first is English language proficiency. So English proficiency is not required, so you don't have to send in your TOEFL, IELTS, or Duolingo scores. Students from your school, English language of instruction, which is this is this exam is basically waived off for all of you. You don't have to send in any of these scores, it is not going to be required. We see that there is SAT and ACT on here, that's only going to be used to meet the English proficiency, which you don't need. So there is no other section on the UC application that will allow you to submit any ACT or SAT score. The total score, right, it is not going to be reviewed. We are test-blind and test-free UC system. None of us are going to review your ACT or SAT scores, not for your admission purpose, not for your scholarship purpose. And none of us are going to look at the scores even if you try to send them in from the College Board. So this is not just for the Merced or any particular campus, but all of us, the UCs, are not reviewing any test scores at all. Next slide, Aastha. So this is basically your AP exam. So if you're trying to add on your AP exam, so international exam, this is the area where you can add them in. So this is this is for exam only, not for your AP, IB coursework. For the ones you're planning to take, for the ones you already have scores for. So this is something that you can add exams that you have taken or you already have score for. Next slide, Aastha. So this is also IB exams, wherein you just have to fill in that you are taking IB or not, and if you have the scores, you can just add on to it. Next slide. So this is your international exams. So people we in India have CBSE, GCSE, ICSE, O Level, so this is for them as well. So you just have to add on. Next slide, if you can just turn the next slide, Aastha. So if you can see that you can just choose India, which year, and you just have to add the exam type, and you just have to then continue, Save and continue. Yeah, so this is how it will be. So if you have received the score, you can just add on your score; if you have the predicted, not received, accordingly you can just add on to it. Next one. So, activities and awards. So your application is—yeah, so your application is just not your academics only. There is this activities and award section that is reviewed and considered as well. So first, this whole section allows you to add up up to 20 different activities across six different categories. So up to 20 activities does not mean that we require you to add all the 20 activities, okay? So it's not that you have to add 20 activities, it's just that you also we allow you to add up to 20. If you don't have 20, that's completely fine. You only have to add five activities if you have, as meaningful to you, and add those five activities and that's completely fine. Remember, it is not the quantity, it is the quality over the quantity of your activities that we look forward to. If there are unfamiliar acronyms, so don't expect us to understand them. Make sure you spell the entire acronym when you list down all the activities. When you're putting in this section, how should you list your activities or your awards? That's very important. So my recommendation and tip is that you put them in the order of your how meaningful it is to you and how much time you have committed to that activity instead of listing it in order of time, suppose, I say. It is not chronological. We do prefer you to list it in the order of importance, which is very, very important. So order of importance to you, how meaningful it is this activity to you. If you have committed to an activity or extracurricular program for the entire 4 years of your high school or career, you know you commit a lot of hours every week to that activity. So that takes is something which is very meaningful and probably very, you know, important to you. So the okay, so list in order of the importance and the order of the ones that's more meaningful to you, not chronological. That's the tip. Second, slide continued. So here it is very important that you focus on the on your roles and responsibilities and why you accomplished them, not just describing the activity, what the activity is. Don't forget if you're planning to do that even after you graduate, after you say suppose you complete your 12th grade, you can also check in check that off in the section here. So we won't ask you any certification or completion, we don't verify that with your school, we don't ask for any awards for you to send that in, but we that but do remember that we also have signed the verification of statement of integrity, that is application integrity. So everything that you list in this section should be something that you have completed and should be something that you are able to talk about, that you have accomplished and that is meaningful to you. So then the activities and awards... The third is basically the activities and awards of educational prep programs. That's how going to be listed in according to it. Slide three, Aastha. Yeah. So as for scholarship and support programs, we don't have any specific link to it. It's just that it's a merit-based thing. So Aastha, Beauti, and I have also discussed that we have a separate scholarship that will be automatically given to you once you fill in your application."
